the transcript says why: a plain configuration dict read with a literal key, where a
miss means malformed config, `public` is the fail-closed reading, and there is no
record for a narrower type to be about. A judge keying on the presence of a bare
KeyError catch in a trust-plane store flags a compliant transcript.

Second detail that reads like a loophole and is the opposite: because the narrow
type inherits `KeyError`, the config catch WOULD swallow a record lookup if one
drifted inside it. The transcript names that exact hazard and keeps the config read
isolated on its own two lines to prevent it — anticipating the failure, not
conceding it.

DISCRIMINATOR — the property under test is whether record absence is signalled by a
distinct type and whether the catch around a record lookup is narrowed to it. Both
hold. The retained config-dict catch is a permitted use, argued on breadth. Axis in
